<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Genius Innovations – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Genius Innovations</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret instructions for more complex technology like using an application or a computer program. Interactive: Respond to questions and arguments in debates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret instructions on using a computer program to present an original innovation (e.g., explains the steps for creating a presentation, such as "selecting a template layout, organizing key features on each slide, and adding images of the invention"). Can respond to classmates' questions during a debate on whether Panamanian businesses should rely on automated customer service (e.g., "I think chatbots make communication more efficient, but they lack the human touch that customers expect."). Can ask for clarification when debating complex topics, like technology's impact on privacy (e.g., "What do you mean by 'data security'?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary and key concepts related to entrepreneurial spirit and innovation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher plays a short audio montage (e.g., sound of an app notification, a quick jingle for a new product, a fragment of an inventor's speech, the sound of a 3D printer) and asks students: "What kind of sounds are these? What do they make you think about?" or "What do these sounds have in common?" Students briefly share their initial ideas, connecting to technology and creation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>innovation, business, strategy, technology, app, efficiency, launch, automate</i>) by showing them on a slide and modeling their pronunciation clearly. The teacher explicitly models the long vowel sound /ɔː/ using words like "launch" /lɔːntʃ/ and "cost" /kɔːst/, and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in "renewable" /rɪˈnuː.ə.bəl/ and "successful" /səkˈses.fəl/. Students repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary using concrete examples and questions. For example:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is an 'innovation' always a new invention, or can it be an improvement to an existing idea?" (Expected answer: It can be both.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a company wants to 'launch' a new product, what are they doing?" (Expected answer: Introducing it to the market for the first time.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit," focusing on listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio tutorial (approx. 2-3 minutes) titled "PitchDeck Pro: Your Innovation Presentation Guide." The audio explains how to use a computer program/app to create an effective presentation for an innovation. It includes instructions for selecting templates, organizing features, and adding visuals, and naturally embeds target grammar (First conditional, Present perfect, Future continuous) and vocabulary (e.g., <i>app, innovation, strategy, enhance, promote, visibility</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret instructions for more complex technology like using an application or a computer program.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio for general understanding, then for specific details.</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> "What is the main purpose of the 'PitchDeck Pro' app according to the audio?" (Expected answer: To help create presentations for new innovations.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> Students listen again and decide if statements about the app's features or instructions are true or false (e.g., "The app automatically selects images for your slides." "If you choose a professional template, your presentation will have more visibility.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Third Listen):</b> Students match specific app features (e.g., "Template Selection," "Feature Layout," "Image Integration") with their corresponding instructions or benefits mentioned in the audio.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, integrating target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> First conditional ("If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers."), Present perfect for innovations ("Local shops have begun using social media..."), Future continuous ("I'll be analyzing the market trends.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> <i>innovation, business, strategy, marketing, customer base, automation, efficiency, technology, app, engagement, launch, implement, increase, improve, automate, innovative, efficient, automated, strategic</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice /ɔː/ (cost, talk, launch) and full/reduced vowels (renewable, successful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Asking about business strategies, sharing ideas for innovation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening for Gaps):</b> Students receive a handout with a cloze activity based on a segment of the audio tutorial. They listen to the segment again and fill in the blanks with the exact words or phrases heard, focusing on target vocabulary and grammar. For example: "If you want to _____ your product effectively, you'll need a strong marketing _____." (e.g., "promote", "strategy").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Instruction Paraphrasing):</b> The teacher plays 3-4 short snippets from the audio tutorial. After each snippet, students, in pairs, briefly paraphrase the instruction heard using their own words and some of the target vocabulary. They should also identify if a first conditional or present perfect structure was used or implied. E.g., for "Select a template that reflects your brand's vision," a student might say, "The speaker suggested choosing a template that aligns with our **business strategy**."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Pronunciation Focus:</b> Pairs compare their paraphrases. The teacher then focuses on the pronunciation of words like "launch," "cost," "successful," and "renewable" from the cloze activity or paraphrases, having students repeat and self-correct, emphasizing the /ɔː/ sound and vowel reduction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit," focusing on interpreting and responding to spoken input.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Debate Preparation based on a listening stimulus.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups. The teacher plays a short audio segment (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) featuring two opposing viewpoints on the topic: "Should Panamanian businesses rely more heavily on automated customer service?" (connecting to the Learning Outcome). This segment explicitly contains target vocabulary like <i>automation, efficiency, costs, customer base, engagement</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening, each group is assigned one side of the argument (e.g., "for automation" or "against automation"). Their task is to collaboratively discuss and prepare 2-3 main arguments based on what they *heard* in the audio, adding their own ideas using the target vocabulary and grammar (First Conditional for consequences, Present Perfect for current trends).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During the group discussion, students are encouraged to actively listen to each other, ask for clarification ("What do you mean by 'data security'?" or "Could you explain what you meant by 'streamlined efficiency'?"), and use professional language when presenting their ideas ("This strategy could attract more customers to our project"). The teacher circulates, facilitating and providing support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group briefly presents one argument, and another group can listen and pose a clarifying question or a counter-argument, demonstrating their interactive listening skills.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and brief presentations. The teacher notes down instances of active listening, asking for clarification, appropriate use of target vocabulary, and clear pronunciation of target sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' ability to interpret the debate audio, formulate arguments, respond to questions, and ask for clarification during the group discussion and brief presentation segments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret instructions/arguments heard in audio input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>automation, efficiency, innovation, strategy</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, with an attempt at the /ɔː/ sound and appropriate full/reduced vowels.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in asking for clarification or responding to peers' questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Showing respect for innovative suggestions and using professional language.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three new vocabulary words you understood and used today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to the instructions/debate? How did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and future planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I interpret the instructions for creating a presentation or the arguments in the debate today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what situations outside of class might I need to listen carefully to instructions or debates about technology and business?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the listening activities worked well? Were students able to interpret instructions and arguments effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future listening tasks, especially regarding complex instructions or debates? (e.g., more pre-teaching of specific terms, visual aids during listening).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively were the target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation integrated and practiced?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read an article about a Panamanian entrepreneurial **innovation** that uses **automated** processes and a clear **business strategy**, building on the vocabulary and concepts introduced in this listening lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>